## I. THE IDEOLOGICAL BLIND SPOT: CONFESSING TO HORIZONTAL COORDINATION IN DAYLIGHT

In traditional smoke-filled industries like oil, airlines, or agricultural commodities, corporate executives understand that coordinating with horizontal competitors to restrict output, withhold product capabilities, boycott downstream rivals, or suppress labor mobility is a federal felony under Section 1 of the Sherman Antitrust Act (15 U.S.C. § 1). Because traditional executives know horizontal collusion carries ten-year prison sentences and billions in civil liability, they hide their agreements and avoid putting them in writing. The leadership of centralized San Francisco AI labs, however, suffers from a fatal psychological and legal blind spot born from their apocalyptic “rationalist” ideology: because they have publicly framed statistical matrix multiplication as an incipient existential threat—memorably summarized by the industry trope that building AI is “summoning the demon,” or Sam Altman’s public remark that *“AI will probably most likely lead to the end of the world, but in the meantime, there’ll be great companies”*—they appear to view horizontal coordination not as an antitrust crime, but as a moral crusade. Operating under this savior framing, competing frontier labs have openly formed cross-company consortia such as the Frontier Model Forum (jointly announced in July 2023), published synchronized “Frontier Safety Frameworks” and “Responsible Scaling Policies,” coordinated capability release thresholds, and utilized aggressive lifetime non-disparagement and vested-equity forfeiture clauses (exposed in May 2024 offboarding document leaks) to control researcher behavior across an insular clique of a few hundred engineers.

In doing so, these executives have walked directly into a catastrophic statutory trap. While joint lobbying of Congress—such as Sam Altman’s May 2023 Senate Judiciary testimony urging federal licensing requirements for compute thresholds—is shielded from antitrust prosecution under the *Noerr-Pennington* doctrine, that immunity stops at the exact boundary of government petitioning. It offers zero protection for private horizontal agreements among competing corporations to restrict market output, coordinate product gating, or standardize capability withholding outside of a binding statute. More importantly, the executives’ primary public justification—that coordinating output restrictions with horizontal competitors is necessary to protect “public safety” or prevent catastrophic risk—was explicitly demolished by the United States Supreme Court nearly half a century ago.

---

## II. *PROFESSIONAL ENGINEERS* (435 U.S. 679): WHY “PUBLIC SAFETY” IS ZERO DEFENSE TO COLLUSION

In *National Society of Professional Engineers v. United States*, 435 U.S. 679 (1978), an association of competing engineering firms enacted a horizontal rule prohibiting members from competing on price prior to project selection, arguing that unfettered competition would force engineers to cut corners, leading to catastrophic bridge collapses and severe harm to public safety. The Supreme Court unanimously crushed this defense, establishing an immutable bedrock rule of American antitrust law: private competitors are strictly forbidden from acting as a shadow legislature and agreeing among themselves that open market competition is “too dangerous” for the public. As the Court held, the Sherman Act reflects a legislative judgment that competition is the fundamental rule of trade; if a specific technology or industry poses genuine physical hazards, only Congress by statute may restrict competition, and any private horizontal pact among competitors to suppress output or gate capabilities in the name of “safety” is illegal on its face.

Every time competing AI labs privately coordinate “capability thresholds,” agree via joint industry forums to delay or withhold open-weight releases, or establish shared private standards that restrict downstream developers from accessing un-throttled inference, they are executing a horizontal output-restriction agreement that is structurally indistinguishable from the cartel struck down in *Professional Engineers*. Furthermore, when two or more frontier labs or their cloud infrastructure backers coordinate to deny API access, synthetic training tokens, or compute clusters to a rising open-weights competitor, they trigger *per se* liability for a horizontal group boycott under *Klor’s, Inc. v. Broadway-Hale Stores, Inc.*, 359 U.S. 207 (1959), and *Fashion Originators’ Guild of America v. FTC*, 312 U.S. 457 (1941)—a landmark case where the Supreme Court explicitly ruled that private industry guilds cannot form extrajudicial cartels to boycott “pirates” or enforce private regulatory codes.

---

## III. DECENTRALIZED ENFORCEMENT: 15 U.S.C. § 15 PRIVATE BOUNTY HUNTERS AND STATE AGS

A common strategic misconception is assuming that frontier AI labs are safe from antitrust liquidation as long as federal executive agencies remain temporarily captured by the “national champion” narrative. That assumption ignores the decentralized, Madisonian architecture of United States antitrust enforcement. Congress intentionally designed the Clayton Act (15 U.S.C. § 15) to bypass federal bureaucratic inertia by creating a private bounty-hunter mechanism: any competing startup, open-weights lab, enterprise customer, or researcher injured in their business or property by a Sherman Act violation may sue the cartel directly in federal district court for **mandatory treble damages** (three times actual damages) plus 100% of reasonable attorneys’ fees.

